<header class="sp-hero">
    <h1>China Visa Guide for Medical Treatment</h1>
    <p>Which visa applies to your passport — and how long you can stay. Most short medical visits (imaging, consultations, second opinions) fit under visa-free entry; longer treatment uses S2 or L.</p>
</header>

<div class="sp-crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/how-it-works.html">Process</a> › <a href="/visa-guide.html">Visa Guide</a></div>

<main class="sp-shell">
    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill">Most relevant for short visits</span>
        <h2>240-Hour (10-Day) Transit Visa-Free</h2>
        <p>Since 17 December 2024, China's transit visa-free policy has been expanded to <strong>240 hours (10 days)</strong> — up from the earlier 72/144-hour rules. For many international patients, this is the simplest path for a short medical visit.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Who qualifies:</strong> citizens of <strong>54 countries</strong> — including the US, UK, Canada, Australia, Singapore, and most of the EU.</li>
            <li><strong>Where you can enter:</strong> <strong>60 designated ports</strong> — covering Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, Shenzhen, Chengdu, Xi'an, Hangzhou, Chongqing, and other major medical cities.</li>
            <li><strong>Where you can travel:</strong> <strong>24 provinces</strong> — the policy now allows cross-province movement within the approved region, so you are not confined to the arrival city.</li>
            <li><strong>What you need:</strong> a passport valid for at least 3 months, plus a <strong>confirmed onward ticket to a third country (or region) within 240 hours</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;"><strong>Fits:</strong> MRI / CT / PET-CT, specialist consultation, written second opinion, and short outpatient procedures — all typically completed within 10 days.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill blue">No onward ticket needed</span>
        <h2>30-Day Unilateral Visa-Free Entry</h2>
        <p>China also grants unilateral visa-free entry (up to 30 days) to citizens of several countries. This is different from transit visa-free: <strong>you do not need an onward ticket to a third country</strong> — you simply arrive and stay up to 30 days.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Australia (trial through 31 December 2026), New Zealand, Japan, South Korea, France, Germany, Italy, Spain, the Netherlands, Malaysia, Switzerland, and others.</li>
            <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> ordinary visits including medical treatment.</li>
            <li><strong>Fit:</strong> initial consultations, imaging, and shorter treatment stays of up to 30 days.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:.85rem;color:#8893a0;">Visa-free lists change — always verify current rules with your nearest Chinese embassy or consulate before booking.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill blue">For longer treatment</span>
        <h2>S2 &amp; L Visas (with Hospital Invitation Letter)</h2>
        <p>If your treatment needs a stay beyond the visa-free windows — or your nationality is not on the visa-free list — you apply for a visa. The two most common for medical travel are the S2 and L visas.</p>
        <table class="visa-table">
            <tr><th>Visa</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Typical stay</th><th>Invitation letter</th></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>S2</strong></td><td>Short-term private visit (incl. medical treatment)</td><td>Up to 180 days</td><td>Hospital can provide</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>L</strong></td><td>Tourism (also used for short medical trips)</td><td>Varies; US citizens qualify for 10-year multiple-entry</td><td>Usually not required for tourism</td></tr>
        </table>
        <h3>How the invitation letter works</h3>
        <p>Once your appointment is confirmed, the hospital can issue an <strong>invitation letter</strong> stating the treatment, dates, and your identity. You submit it with your visa application at a Chinese embassy or visa center. We coordinate this letter as part of hospital matching — you do not chase it alone.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <h2>Which option fits your case?</h2>
        <table class="visa-table">
            <tr><th>Your visit</th><th>Typical duration</th><th>Best option</th></tr>
            <tr><td>MRI / CT / PET-CT or second opinion</td><td>2–7 days</td><td>240h transit visa-free or 30-day visa-free</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Initial consultation + short workup</td><td>3–10 days</td><td>240h transit visa-free or 30-day visa-free</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Major surgery + recovery</td><td>2–6 weeks</td><td>S2 or L visa (with invitation letter)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ongoing treatment (cancer, rehabilitation)</td><td>1–6 months</td><td>S2 visa</td></tr>
        </table>
    </section>

    <h2 class="sp-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="sp-faq">
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Can I enter China without a visa for medical treatment?</h3><p>It depends on your nationality. Citizens of 54 countries can use the 240-hour (10-day) transit visa-free policy with a confirmed onward ticket. Many nationalities also qualify for 30-day unilateral visa-free entry. Otherwise, an S2 or L visa applies.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>What is the 240-hour transit visa-free policy?</h3><p>Since 17 December 2024, eligible citizens of 54 countries transiting China can stay up to 240 hours (10 days) visa-free, entering through 60 designated ports and traveling within 24 provinces — provided they hold a passport valid 3+ months and a confirmed onward ticket within 240 hours.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Can I get an MRI or second opinion under 240-hour transit visa-free?</h3><p>Yes. Most imaging, consultations, and second opinions finish well within 10 days, so transit visa-free entry is a practical option for short medical visits. We confirm your schedule and the applicable visa before you book.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Does the hospital provide an invitation letter?</h3><p>Yes — once your appointment is confirmed, the hospital can provide an invitation letter to support an S2 or L visa application. We coordinate this as part of our hospital matching service.</p></div>
    </div>

